# Two wrappers for the schwa, which is a phoneme of Catalan's own and so has
# none of IBM's. Both are the shape of the 1,037 the module already has: bare,
# so there is no landing place and no ventproc, because a wrapper is entered
# and answers and the choice points around the call belong to whoever planted
# them.
#
# The two they stand beside are ZZtest_string_s_4_1_ZZstring115 and
# ZZlprp_load__insert_2pt_s_4_1_ZZstring115, which carry the phone a; the 4
# is the field the string belongs to and the 1 is how many.

# Whether the phone the scan is on is the schwa.
rule cat_test_schwa takes 1 from glob.obj
  bare
  call test_string_s 4 1 sym cat_ph_schwa
end

# Lay a schwa down between the two ends a letter rule was handed.
rule cat_insert_schwa takes 3 from glob.obj
  bare
  call lpta_rpta_loadp arg 1 arg 2
  call insert_2pt_s 4 1 sym cat_ph_schwa 0
end


# ---- the two open vowels -------------------------------------------------
#
# Getting a grave accent to a rule of its own takes three wrappers, because
# the module has nowhere for one to go. normalize_span_letters flattens every
# character Castilian does not use, and the letter chain has no arm for one
# that survives, so a grave either loses its accent or goes silent. Both are
# reached through wrappers used in exactly one place each, which is what makes
# this cheap: a wrapper is a rule, and a rule of ours in its place is an arm
# taken out of one chain and put into another.

# The two arms of the normaliser that flatten e and o with a grave. Answering
# 1 is what test_string_s answers when the character is not the one asked
# about, so the arm is never taken and the grave comes through as itself.
rule ZZtest_string_s_1_1_ZZstring164 takes 1 from glob.obj
  bare
  answer 1
end

rule ZZtest_string_s_1_1_ZZstring9 takes 1 from glob.obj
  bare
  answer 1
end

# And the arm of the letter chain that Castilian keeps for n with a tilde,
# which Catalan never writes: it spells that sound ny, and apply_span_n_rules
# is where that is done. So the arm is free, and this is what it now asks --
# is the letter either grave? -- with apply_span_enye_rules, which it calls,
# rewritten to say which.
rule ZZtest_string_s_1_1_ZZstring58 takes 1 from glob.obj
  bare
  call test_string_s 1 1 sym cat_letter_egrave
  if answer is 0
    answer 0
  end
  call test_string_s 1 1 sym cat_letter_ograve
end

# The open vowels as phones, for the value chain to test and a letter rule to
# lay down. The same shape as the schwa's pair.
rule cat_test_eopen takes 1 from glob.obj
  bare
  call test_string_s 4 1 sym cat_ph_eopen
end

rule cat_test_oopen takes 1 from glob.obj
  bare
  call test_string_s 4 1 sym cat_ph_oopen
end

# Whether the letter the scan is on is one of the two that make a diphthong
# after a vowel. Catalan's ou and oi are the strongest thing anybody can say
# about an unaccented stressed o: it is open in nou, bou, prou, plou, dijous,
# noia, boira and coure, and the grammars give it as a rule rather than as a
# tendency. The caller sets the scan; answering 0 is a match, which is what
# test_string_s answers and what the letter rules read.
rule cat_glide_letter takes 1 from glob.obj
  bare
  call test_string_s 1 1 sym cat_letter_u
  if answer is 0
    answer 0
  end
  call test_string_s 1 1 sym cat_letter_i
end

# The consonants an open o stands in front of at the end of a word. The
# grammars give a list -- oc, of, oig, ol, ort, ost, ot and the feminines --
# and these two are the part of it that has no exception worth the name: poc,
# groc, foc, joc, xoc, toc, bloc; sol, vol, col, gol, escola, bola, cassola.
# The rest of the list is left alone on purpose. ot would take tot with it,
# which is [t o t] and one of the commonest words in the language, and there
# is nowhere here to keep an exception: a word list is a dictionary and this
# module has none it can add to.
rule cat_open_o_cons takes 1 from glob.obj
  bare
  call test_string_s 1 1 sym cat_letter_c
  if answer is 0
    answer 0
  end
  call test_string_s 1 1 sym cat_letter_l
end

# Whether the word ends after the letter the scan is on, counting the endings
# that are put on a stem without changing it: a feminine a, a plural s, and
# the two together. So escola and escoles reach the same arm groc does.
#
# There is no step in here, and that is the whole of what took the longest to
# see. A test that matches moves the scan past what it matched and a test that
# fails leaves it where it was, so the letter the caller matched has already
# been stepped over by the matching. cat_word_end above is IBM's own rule of
# this shape and says the same thing by having no step in it either: it tests
# for an s and then asks what follows straight away. Stepping by hand as well
# skips a letter, which reads every word as though it were two shorter --
# molt, colze and golfa all came out as though they ended at the l.
rule cat_stem_ends takes 1 from glob.obj
  bare
  call ZZtestFldeq1_3_1                                # anything after it?
  if answer is not 0
    answer 0                                           # groc, sol, vol
  end
  call test_string_s 1 1 sym cat_letter_a
  if answer is 0
    call cat_after_one_more                            # escola, escoles
    if answer is 0
      answer 0
    end
    answer 1
  end
  call test_string_s 1 1 sym cat_letter_e
  if answer is 0
    call cat_after_one_more
    if answer is 0
      answer 0
    end
    answer 1
  end
  call test_string_s 1 1 sym cat_letter_s
  if answer is not 0
    answer 1
  end
  call ZZtestFldeq1_3_1
  if answer is not 0
    answer 0                                           # jocs, sols
  end
  answer 1
end

# The same question, but a stem ending in a vowel and not a bare one: rosa
# and roses, where cos and gos must not follow. It is what -osa needs and
# what -os must not have.
rule cat_stem_ends_vowel takes 1 from glob.obj
  bare
  call ZZtestFldeq1_3_1
  if answer is not 0
    answer 1                                           # cos, gos, os, tros
  end
  call test_string_s 1 1 sym cat_letter_a
  if answer is 0
    call cat_after_one_more
    if answer is 0
      answer 0
    end
    answer 1
  end
  call test_string_s 1 1 sym cat_letter_e
  if answer is 0
    call cat_after_one_more
    if answer is 0
      answer 0
    end
    answer 1
  end
  answer 1
end

# And the same question one letter further on, for the s of escoles.
rule cat_after_one_more takes 1 from glob.obj
  bare
  call ZZtestFldeq1_3_1
  if answer is not 0
    answer 0
  end
  call test_string_s 1 1 sym cat_letter_s
  if answer is not 0
    answer 1
  end
  call ZZtestFldeq1_3_1
  if answer is not 0
    answer 0
  end
  answer 1
end

# The three words whose eu is the close vowel: meu, teu, seu. Everything else
# the language ends in -eu is open -- deu, peu, neu, creu, beu, veu, preu --
# and so is every second person plural in -eu, canteu and beveu and veieu.
# What tells those from teu is that the three close ones are whole words of
# three letters, so the head has to be where the word starts. Without that
# canteu would follow teu, and it does not.
rule cat_close_eu_head takes 1 from glob.obj
  bare
  call test_string_s 1 1 sym cat_letter_m
  if answer is 0
    call cat_word_starts
    if answer is 0
      answer 0
    end
    answer 1
  end
  call test_string_s 1 1 sym cat_letter_t
  if answer is 0
    call cat_word_starts
    if answer is 0
      answer 0
    end
    answer 1
  end
  call test_string_s 1 1 sym cat_letter_s
  if answer is 0
    call cat_word_starts
    if answer is 0
      answer 0
    end
    answer 1
  end
  answer 1
end

# The words whose o is open that no ending here reaches, one test apiece.
#
# This is the list to add to, and adding a word is two lines: the letters that
# follow its o go in rules/constants as one `bytes\' line, and one block like
# the one below comes here with that name in it. The letters before the o are
# not asked about at all where the tail is enough to name the word.
#
# volta and revolta are open where molt is close, which is the same shape and
# the other vowel, and is why this list exists rather than another ending.
rule cat_open_o_word takes 3 from glob.obj
  bare
  call ZZlpta_load__setscan_r__1 arg 2
  if answer is 0
    call test_string_s 1 3 sym cat_tail_lta
    if answer is 0
      call cat_after_one_more
      if answer is 0
        answer 0
      end
    end
  end
  # Where the tail is one letter it names too many words on its own, so the
  # letter in front comes into it and the word has to start there. cor and
  # por against mar and clar; got against tot, which is close.
  call ZZlpta_load__setscan_r__1 arg 2
  if answer is 0
    call test_string_s 1 1 sym cat_tail_r
    if answer is 0
      call cat_after_one_more
      if answer is 0
        call ZZlpta_load__setscan_l__1 arg 1
        if answer is 0
          call test_string_s 1 1 sym cat_letter_c
          if answer is 0
            call cat_word_starts
            if answer is 0
              answer 0
            end
          end
        end
      end
    end
  end
  call ZZlpta_load__setscan_r__1 arg 2
  if answer is 0
    call test_string_s 1 1 sym cat_tail_t
    if answer is 0
      call cat_after_one_more
      if answer is 0
        call ZZlpta_load__setscan_l__1 arg 1
        if answer is 0
          call test_string_s 1 1 sym cat_letter_g
          if answer is 0
            call cat_word_starts
            if answer is 0
              answer 0
            end
          end
        end
      end
    end
  end
  answer 1
end

# The letters that stand in front of an open o before an n: b for bon and
# bona, d for dona and dones. This is the list to add a word to when the word
# ends in -on or -ona, and adding one is one more test here.
rule cat_open_on_head takes 1 from glob.obj
  bare
  call test_string_s 1 1 sym cat_letter_b
  if answer is 0
    answer 0
  end
  call test_string_s 1 1 sym cat_letter_d
end

# Whether the letter the scan is on is a g or a j, which after an n is the
# same sound spelt two ways: taronja and taronges are one word.
rule cat_soft_gj takes 1 from glob.obj
  bare
  call test_string_s 1 1 sym cat_letter_g
  if answer is 0
    answer 0
  end
  call test_string_s 1 1 sym cat_letter_j
end

# Whether the word starts where the scan is standing, which is the same
# question the r rule asks at the other end: what comes before the first
# letter of a word is not a letter.
rule cat_word_starts takes 1 from glob.obj
  bare
  call ZZtestFldeq1_3_1
  if answer is not 0
    answer 0
  end
  answer 1
end

# The three infinitives a word-final r goes from that the walk cannot find.
# apply_span_r_rules drops the r of a word with a second vowel in it -- passar
# is [p @ s a], carrer [k @ R e] -- and fer, dir and ser have one vowel each,
# so they came out with the r on. What keeps clar, cor, mar and por is exactly
# the test they fail, so there is nothing to loosen: it is these three words
# and no rule above them, and they are written down as themselves.
#
# The vowel is asked about as well as the consonant, and that is what keeps
# far, dur and sor: f-e-r, s-e-r and d-i-r are the only Catalan words of those
# three shapes. The caller has set the scan leftwards and stepped over the
# vowel is what the other arm does; this steps over it itself, since a test
# that matches moves the scan and a field test does not.
rule cat_mono_infinitive takes 1 from glob.obj
  bare
  call test_string_s 1 1 sym cat_letter_e
  if answer is 0
    call test_string_s 1 1 sym cat_letter_f
    if answer is 0
      call cat_word_starts
      if answer is 0
        answer 0
      end
      answer 1
    end
    call test_string_s 1 1 sym cat_letter_s
    if answer is 0
      call cat_word_starts
      if answer is 0
        answer 0
      end
      answer 1
    end
    answer 1
  end
  call test_string_s 1 1 sym cat_letter_i
  if answer is 0
    call test_string_s 1 1 sym cat_letter_d
    if answer is 0
      call cat_word_starts
      if answer is 0
        answer 0
      end
      answer 1
    end
    answer 1
  end
  # por and dur, which are not infinitives and go the same way. cor and mar
  # keep their r and clar and rar with them, so this is four words asked
  # about by both their letters and not an ending.
  call test_string_s 1 1 sym cat_letter_o
  if answer is 0
    call test_string_s 1 1 sym cat_letter_p
    if answer is 0
      call cat_word_starts
      if answer is 0
        answer 0
      end
      answer 1
    end
    answer 1
  end
  call test_string_s 1 1 sym cat_letter_u
  if answer is 0
    call test_string_s 1 1 sym cat_letter_d
    if answer is 0
      call cat_word_starts
      if answer is 0
        answer 0
      end
      answer 1
    end
  end
  answer 1
end

# Whether the word this letter is in has ended, and the step over the boundary
# if it has. What stands between two words is a token that is not a letter, so
# the step is the same one the walks inside a word take.
rule cat_step_over_boundary takes 1 from glob.obj
  bare
  call ZZtestFldeq1_3_1                        # still inside this word?
  if answer is 0
    answer 1
  end
  call advance_tok                             # over the boundary
  if answer is not 0
    answer 1
  end
  call ZZtestFldeq1_3_1                        # and a letter after it?
  if answer is not 0
    answer 1
  end
  answer 0
end

# Whether the sound that begins the next word is voiced.
#
# Two tests rather than one, and the reason is the whole of what this took.
# A field test reads the token the scan is standing on and a string test reads
# the characters through the scan itself, and advance_tok moves the first and
# not the second: after the step over the boundary, asking whether the letter
# is a vowel answers about the next word and asking whether it is a d answers
# about nothing. So the caller saves where the scan got to and sets it again
# from there, and both kinds of test then agree. Nothing in IBM's Spanish ever
# stepped past the end of the word it was reading, which is why no rule here
# had shown it.
rule cat_next_word_voiced takes 1 from glob.obj
  bare
  call ZZtestFldeq1_4_1                        # a vowel begins the word
  if answer is 0
    answer 0
  end
  call cat_voiced_cons
end

# Whether a vowel begins the next word, an h in front of it counting for
# nothing. The caller has stepped over the boundary and set the scan again.
# h is silent in Catalan, so amb homes has a vowel after amb exactly as amb
# ells does, and it is the only letter that has to be seen through.
rule cat_next_word_vowel takes 1 from glob.obj
  bare
  call ZZtestFldeq1_4_1
  if answer is 0
    answer 0
  end
  call test_string_s 1 1 sym cat_letter_h
  if answer is not 0
    answer 1
  end
  call ZZtestFldeq1_4_1
  if answer is 0
    answer 0
  end
  answer 1
end

# The letters Catalan begins a word with that carry voice. h is in because it
# says nothing: amb homes is amb followed by a vowel. The voiceless ones --
# c, f, p, q, s, t, x -- and the vowels are asked about elsewhere.
rule cat_voiced_cons takes 1 from glob.obj
  bare
  call test_string_s 1 1 sym cat_letter_b
  if answer is 0
    answer 0
  end
  call test_string_s 1 1 sym cat_letter_d
  if answer is 0
    answer 0
  end
  call test_string_s 1 1 sym cat_letter_g
  if answer is 0
    answer 0
  end
  call test_string_s 1 1 sym cat_letter_j
  if answer is 0
    answer 0
  end
  call test_string_s 1 1 sym cat_letter_l
  if answer is 0
    answer 0
  end
  call test_string_s 1 1 sym cat_letter_m
  if answer is 0
    answer 0
  end
  call test_string_s 1 1 sym cat_letter_n
  if answer is 0
    answer 0
  end
  call test_string_s 1 1 sym cat_letter_r
  if answer is 0
    answer 0
  end
  call test_string_s 1 1 sym cat_letter_v
  if answer is 0
    answer 0
  end
  call test_string_s 1 1 sym cat_letter_z
end

# The m a word-final b after an m is said as. st_phone.up says why it is this
# rather than the mute phone or nothing at all.
rule cat_insert_m takes 3 from glob.obj
  bare
  call lpta_rpta_loadp arg 1 arg 2
  call insert_2pt_s 4 1 sym cat_ph_m 0
end

rule cat_insert_eopen takes 3 from glob.obj
  bare
  call lpta_rpta_loadp arg 1 arg 2
  call insert_2pt_s 4 1 sym cat_ph_eopen 0
end

rule cat_insert_oopen takes 3 from glob.obj
  bare
  call lpta_rpta_loadp arg 1 arg 2
  call insert_2pt_s 4 1 sym cat_ph_oopen 0
end

# Whether the letter the scan is on is one Catalan says without voice. A
# stop before one of these devoices exactly as it does at the end of a word --
# dissabte is [d i s a p t @], dubte [d u p t @], capser [k a p s e] -- and
# the three stop rules ask this once rather than carrying five tests each.
#
# The caller sets the scan; a test that fails leaves it where it was, so the
# five can be asked one after another. Answering 0 is a match, which is what
# test_string_s answers and what the rules that call this read.
rule cat_voiceless_follows takes 1 from glob.obj
  bare
  call ZZtest_string_s_1_1_ZZstring104                 # t
  if answer is 0
    answer 0
  end
  call ZZtest_string_s_1_1_ZZstring96                  # s
  if answer is 0
    answer 0
  end
  call ZZtest_string_s_1_1_ZZstring20                  # c
  if answer is 0
    answer 0
  end
  call ZZtest_string_s_1_1_ZZstring90                  # p
  if answer is 0
    answer 0
  end
  call ZZtest_string_s_1_1_ZZstring48                  # f
end

# Nothing at all, laid down where Castilian lays down a joiner.
#
# Castilian writes a two-digit number as three words -- cuarenta y cinco --
# and convert_span_double puts the space and the y in between with two calls.
# Catalan writes the same number as one: quarantacinc, vinticinc. So the two
# call sites in that rule are pointed at these, which take what they are given
# and lay down nothing, and the tens word and the unit meet with no boundary
# between them.
#
# The first lays down the mark alone, which joins the two halves without a
# space and keeps the rules that ask about neighbouring letters from reading
# across it. The second lays down nothing, which is where the y went.
rule cat_insert_join takes 1 from glob.obj
  bare
  call insert_l 1 1 sym cat_num_join 0
end

rule cat_insert_nothing_given takes 2 from glob.obj
  bare
  answer 0
end


# ---- the arm that made an ll into a y ------------------------------------

# Castilian is yeista: its ll is /j/ and between two vowels it is the
# approximant, so span_phrase_level_rules has an arm that rewrites the phone L
# into y when the word before ends in a sonorant. Catalan's ll is a lateral
# wherever it stands -- la llum is [l @ L u m] and not [l @ y u m], el llop
# [@ l L O p] -- and that arm was making it a y at every word boundary after a
# vowel, which is most of the times the language writes one.
#
# The arm is reached through this wrapper and through no other call in the
# module, so a wrapper that never matches is that arm taken out of the chain,
# the same way the two graves were freed above. Answering 1 is what test_synch
# answers when the mark it is asked about is not there.
rule ZZtest_synch20_ZZstring77 takes 1 from glob.obj
  bare
  answer 1
end


# ---- a word-final r, which Central Catalan does not say -------------------

# passar is [p @ s a], carrer [k @ R e], amor [@ m u]; clar, cor and mar keep
# theirs. What tells the two apart is not the ending but the word: an r at the
# end goes when there is a second vowel behind it, which is why every one of
# the three that keep it is a syllable long.
#
# The letters are where this can be asked, because a letter rule is handed the
# run it is reading and the scan will walk either way from it. What cannot be
# done there is drop the r outright: assign_span_stress decides between the
# last syllable and the one before it by looking at the last phone, and a word
# that ended in a vowel would take the stress a syllable back -- passar came
# out [p a s @], which is a different word. So the r becomes the phone
# nothing here can reach instead, and ss_val.up makes that silent: the word
# still ends in a consonant when the stress is placed, and says nothing when
# it is spoken.

# Whether the r the letter rule is reading is the last sound of its word.
# Three things count as the end: nothing to the right at all, something that
# is not a letter, or an s that is itself the last -- carrers is [k @ R e s]
# and dinars [d i n a s], because the plural is added to a word that had
# already lost its r.
rule cat_word_end takes 2 from glob.obj
  bare
  call ZZlpta_load__setscan_r__1 arg 1
  if answer is not 0
    answer 0
  end
  call ZZtestFldeq1_3_1                                # a letter after it?
  if answer is not 0
    answer 0
  end
  call ZZtest_string_s_1_1_ZZstring96                  # an s?
  if answer is not 0
    answer 1
  end
  call ZZtestFldeq1_3_1                                # and nothing after that?
  if answer is not 0
    answer 0
  end
  answer 1
end

# One step of the walk leftwards over the letters: nought where the step
# found a vowel and one where it did not. A test that matches leaves the scan
# where it was, so the letter has to be stepped over by hand; advance_tok is
# what does that, and it answers the other way round from the tests, nought
# for a step taken.
#
# A step that has run out of word does not move, so asking again asks about
# the same place and answers the same. That is what lets the caller below
# repeat this a fixed number of times without having to be told to stop.
rule cat_vowel_step takes 1 from glob.obj
  bare
  call ZZtestFldeq1_3_1                                # still inside the word?
  if answer is not 0
    answer 1
  end
  call ZZtestFldeq1_4_1                                # a vowel?
  if answer is 0
    answer 0
  end
  call advance_tok
  answer 1
end

# Whether there is another vowel behind the one the caller has stopped on.
# Written out rather than looped: the notation's while is a backtracking loop
# and wants a choice point, which a bare rule has no business planting -- the
# ones around the call belong to the letter rule that made it. Eight steps is
# further than any Catalan word puts two vowels apart; constructor has three
# letters between its last two and transgressor three.
rule cat_more_vowels_left takes 1 from glob.obj
  bare
  call cat_vowel_step
  if answer is 0
    answer 0
  end
  call cat_vowel_step
  if answer is 0
    answer 0
  end
  call cat_vowel_step
  if answer is 0
    answer 0
  end
  call cat_vowel_step
  if answer is 0
    answer 0
  end
  call cat_vowel_step
  if answer is 0
    answer 0
  end
  call cat_vowel_step
  if answer is 0
    answer 0
  end
  call cat_vowel_step
  if answer is 0
    answer 0
  end
  call cat_vowel_step
  if answer is 0
    answer 0
  end
  answer 1
end

# The phone a dropped r becomes, laid down between the two ends the letter
# rule was handed. The same shape as the schwa's inserter above.
rule cat_insert_mute takes 3 from glob.obj
  bare
  call lpta_rpta_loadp arg 1 arg 2
  call insert_2pt_s 4 1 sym cat_ph_mute 0
end


# Whether the phone the scan is on is the n a Catalan word ends with, which
# is the interdental's code wearing an n's record. ss_val.up says why.
# And the voiced s, for the same reason one letter along. st_phone.up voices
# a word-final s in front of the next word, and assign_span_stress asks what
# the last phone is by its code: an s with a vowel before it takes the
# penultimate syllable and a z is not that code, so escoles said on its own
# was [@ s k O l @ s] and escoles i taules was [@ s k u l e z], which is a
# different word. It is the same trap the final n fell into and it is caught
# in the same place, st_strss.up.
rule cat_test_final_z takes 1 from glob.obj
  bare
  call test_string_s 4 1 sym cat_ph_z
end

rule cat_test_final_n takes 1 from glob.obj
  bare
  call test_string_s 4 1 sym cat_ph_final_n
end


# ---- j and g before e or i, which harden after a nasal or a lateral -------

# Catalan says the voiced postalveolar as a fricative on its own -- jove is
# [Z O B @], gener [Z @ n e] -- and as an affricate directly after a nasal or
# a lateral: penjat is [p @ N d Z a t], menjar [m @ N d Z a], mengen
# [m e N d Z @ n], angel [a N d Z @ l]. It is the same sound tg and tj already
# spell in metge and platja, which is why the bytes it lays down are the ones
# rules/constants already holds for those.
#
# The letter is where this can be asked, because a letter rule is handed the
# run it is reading and the scan will walk either way from it. What it cannot
# see is the word before, so el jove keeps the fricative where a Catalan
# speaker would harden it; that is a phrase-level rule and this is not it.

# Whether the letter the scan has been set to is one of the two that harden a
# following j. The caller sets the scan; a test that fails leaves it where it
# was, so the two can be asked one after the other, and answering 0 is a match
# as test_string_s answers it.
rule cat_nasal_lateral_here takes 1 from glob.obj
  bare
  call ZZtest_string_s_1_1_ZZstring84                  # an n
  if answer is 0
    answer 0
  end
  call ZZtest_string_s_1_1_ZZstring69                  # or an l
end

# Lay the voiced postalveolar down between the two ends a letter rule was
# handed: two phones after a nasal or a lateral and one everywhere else.
#
# The scan is set leftwards from the left-hand end, which is the letter before
# the one being read. A scan that cannot be set is the start of the word, and
# there is nothing there to harden against.
rule cat_insert_zz takes 3 from glob.obj
  bare
  call ZZlpta_load__setscan_l__1 arg 1
  if answer is 0
    call cat_nasal_lateral_here
    if answer is 0
      call lpta_rpta_loadp arg 1 arg 2
      call insert_2pt_s 4 2 sym cat_ph_dzz 0
      answer answer
    end
  end
  call lpta_rpta_loadp arg 1 arg 2
  call insert_2pt_s 4 1 sym cat_ph_zz 0
end


# Whether what follows the letter the scan has been set to is the sound j and
# g before e and i say. An n in front of it is the palatal nasal rather than
# the alveolar one -- penjat is [p @ N d Z a t] and mengen [m e N d Z @ n],
# which is the same assimilation Castilian makes in front of its own ch and
# which Catalan spelling reaches through two more letters.
#
# A test that matches steps the scan past what it matched, so the e and the i
# are asked about the letter after the g; one that fails leaves it where it
# was, so the two can be asked one after the other.
rule cat_palatal_follows takes 1 from glob.obj
  bare
  call ZZtest_string_s_1_1_ZZstring64                  # a j?
  if answer is 0
    answer 0
  end
  call ZZtest_string_s_1_1_ZZstring54                  # or a g,
  if answer is not 0
    answer 1
  end
  call ZZtest_string_s_1_1_ZZstring77                  # with an e after it?
  if answer is 0
    answer 0
  end
  call ZZtest_string_s_1_1_ZZstring109                 # or an i?
end
